# OOP Object Interaction Demo

## Learning Goals

By the end of this demo, students should be able to:

- explain the difference between a class and an object
- identify attributes and methods
- explain that each object has its own attribute values
- describe how a constructor creates a new object
- explain how method calls can read or update object state
- write exam-style explanations of object-oriented programming concepts

## Key Idea

In object-oriented programming, a class is a blueprint.

An object is an instance created from the class.

A class can define:

```text
attributes = data stored by the object
methods = actions the object can perform
```

Two objects made from the same class can have the same attributes and methods, but different values.

