## Exam-style Explanation

A class defines the attributes and methods that its objects will have. An object is an instance of a class. Each object has its own attribute values, so changing an attribute in one object does not automatically change the same attribute in another object. Methods are called on objects and can access or update the attributes of that object.

## Common Mistakes

| Mistake | Why It Is Wrong | Better Understanding |
|---|---|---|
| thinking class and object are the same | a class is a blueprint; an object is an instance | one class can create many objects |
| thinking all objects share the same attribute values | each object stores its own state | different objects can have different balances |
| confusing attributes and methods | attributes store data; methods perform actions | use clear terminology |
| forgetting constructor role | constructor sets initial values | object creation often calls a constructor |
| thinking a method changes every object | a method call usually affects the selected object | object state is separate |

## Quick Practice

### Question 1

What is the difference between a class and an object?

<details>
<summary>Answer</summary>

A class is a blueprint or template. An object is an instance created from that class.

</details>

### Question 2

In a `BankAccount` class, is `balance` more likely to be an attribute or a method?

<details>
<summary>Answer</summary>

`balance` is an attribute because it stores data about the object.

</details>

### Question 3

Why does depositing money into one account object not change another account object?

<details>
<summary>Answer</summary>

Each object has its own attribute values. The deposit method updates the balance of the object it is called on, not every object of the class.

</details>
